WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.800
Reflection sends part of the wave energy back from an interface.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.760
Refraction changes the direction of transmitted energy when the wave enters

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.200
material with a different seismic velocity.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.740
For the source’s downward-traveling ray, entering faster material bends the ray

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.540
toward a shallower angle to a horizontal interface.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.320
Entering slower material bends it closer to the interface normal.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.900
Wave speed depends on elastic properties and density together.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.580
A general increase in mantle velocity with depth is not a

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.120
rule that density alone always makes every wave faster.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.120
Near an earthquake, the shorter path through crust can produce the

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.180
first arrival. Farther away, a path that enters faster mantle can

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.520
arrive before the slower crustal path despite its greater length.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.420
The distance where the first arrival changes constrains crustal thickness when

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.380
combined with velocities and a model.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.740
The Moho is the crust–mantle boundary.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.160
A reflected travel time includes the trip down and back up.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.800
For the textbook’s idealized 6 km/s crust, a 10-second vertical round

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.500
trip corresponds to 30 km depth, not 60 km.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:10.060
Compressional waves, called P-waves, refract strongly at the core–mantle boundary, leaving

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.000
a zone without direct P arrivals.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.900
A P-wave shadow is not evidence that P-waves cannot travel through

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.460
liquid. Shear waves, called S-waves, do not cross the liquid outer

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.240
core. The broad S-wave shadow, together with other observations, supports a

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.760
molten outer core. Reflected and converted waves can take indirect paths.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.800
Keep direct-wave shadow claims separate from the claim that no seismic

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.500
energy of any kind could ever be detected there.
